      title: "Newspapers: a mail-order ad pays the first time, a retail ad needs frequency",
      minutes: 7,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 36 · Writing for Different Media · Newspapers · pp. 283–286 (PDF 308–311)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Putting an ad in a paper only once is simply a waste of time and money.", page: "283" },
        { text: "I found that consistency was my most important principle.", page: "285" }
      ],
      idea: "Sugarman speaks from both sides of the desk here. He advertised in many dailies and weeklies, and he published the *Maui Weekly* for six years before selling it to the island's daily in 2005.\n\n**Two different rules.** A **mail-order** ad in a newspaper should pay for itself, covering the cost of the space, the first time it runs. A **retail** ad (a restaurant, a store) needs **frequency**, and running it once wastes time and money (first quote). People don't respond right away. Trust builds after they've seen you several times, or the timing was just wrong the first week because of other things happening in town. His restaurant example: 13 weeks in the dining section, almost nothing in week 1, a few more customers in week 2, then steadily more.\n\n**The exception that proves the rule.** In college at the University of Miami, a barbecue owner asked him how to bring in students. He ran an ad in the campus paper for a chicken-and-rib special with a catch: to get the price, you had to bring in the top of a Brinks armored car. When he finally drove over, there was a line around the block, students holding plywood, sheets of tin and garbage-can lids labeled as armored-car tops. It worked without frequency because it spoke to students the way they liked to be spoken to (trigger #11, Nature of Prospect) and turned the offer into a game they could play (trigger #1, Involvement).\n\n**Match the paper to the price.** Big-city dailies reach the mass market, so they suit lower-priced items. High-ticket products belong in an upscale paper; his JS&A was at one point among the *Wall Street Journal*'s largest advertisers. The *Journal* also gave him speed: ads in within days and results a few days later, which fit electronics that rose, peaked and went obsolete fast. Buy space right (brokers can get it cheap). A small ad saves cash and suits a low-price offer, but you're competing with other small ads instead of owning the page.\n\n**Consistency.** At the *Maui Weekly* he fixed one format and kept it week after week (second quote, trigger #15). Readers came to look forward to it, and the same rule worked for his advertisers: a regular schedule and the same format, with the copy changing.\n\n**Newspapers' advantages:** fast response (a daily lives a few days, a weekly about 10), frequency for restaurants, car dealers, furniture, clothing and real estate, and so many papers that a winning ad can roll out to millions quickly.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Mail-order ad: it must pay for itself the first time it runs",
        "Retail ad: frequency is the key, because trust builds over weeks",
        "The Brinks armored-car-top ad worked without frequency because it spoke the students' language",
        "Match the publication to the price: mass dailies for low-priced items, upscale papers for high-ticket",
        "Keep one format and change the copy: consistency built the Maui Weekly and its advertisers",
        "A newspaper's edge is speed: in fast, response fast"
      ],
      why: "ZDub runs both kinds of ads. Lead-gen ads should be judged on whether they pay for themselves; local presence for a trade or PMT needs frequency and a consistent look, and clients have to be told not to judge it after one week.",
