      pdfPages: "311–312",
      title: "Billboards: one big idea someone can read at 75 miles an hour",
      minutes: 5,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 36 · Writing for Different Media · Billboards · pp. 286–287 (PDF 311–312)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Here is where simplicity is the controlling factor.", page: "286" },
        { text: "But whatever the task, the key is brevity and simplicity.", page: "287" }
      ],
      idea: "A billboard uses so few words that they must be very powerful or create instant awareness; otherwise the board is useless. From your research you write **one headline**, at most a minimal subheadline, and a **graphic that carries the big idea**. That's all.\n\n**Simplicity controls everything here** (first quote; trigger #24). The message has to be so simple and direct that people in cars passing at 75 miles an hour can pick it out and grasp its meaning. You have a few seconds to capture the imagination.\n\n**Billboards can build curiosity.** His example is the Folgers coffee teaser campaign of the late 1960s when Folgers entered Chicago: billboards announced that a mountain was moving to Chicago. The mountain was the picture on the coffee can, but nobody knew what the boards meant until the official announcement. That's trigger #19 (Curiosity) and his seeds of curiosity (Ch 10) stretched across a whole campaign: open a question, hold back the answer, then pay it off.\n\n**What billboards are for:** creating buzz (Folgers), building brand recognition and awareness, keeping a known brand in sight, pointing people to a business up the road, or selling beer. Whatever the job, brevity and simplicity are the key (second quote).\n\n**Why this matters more today than in the book.** Sugarman wrote this section about roadside signs, but the conditions he describes (few words, passing speed, a few seconds) are exactly how people meet a Meta static ad, the first frame of a Reel, a Story, or a contractor's truck wrap at a stoplight. In those places, his billboard rules apply in full:\n- one idea, not three\n- a picture that does most of the work\n- a headline short enough to read at a glance\n- every other detail moved to the next step (primary text, landing page)\n\nThis connects back to Axiom 2: all the graphic elements exist to get the first sentence read. On a billboard, the headline *is* the whole message, so the 'first sentence' has to be the idea itself.",
      keyPoints: [
        "A billboard is a headline and a picture; nothing else gets read",
        "One big idea, simple enough to grasp at 75 miles an hour",
        "Teasers work: Folgers' 'mountain moving to Chicago' built buzz before the reveal",
        "Billboards suit buzz, brand awareness, or a business just up the road",
        "Brevity and simplicity are the whole craft"
      ],
      why: "A Meta thumb-stopper, a Story frame and a truck wrap are all billboards: people pass at scroll or road speed. If the idea can't be grasped in about three seconds, the rest of the ad never gets a chance.",
