      id: "aw-036e",
      part: "s4",
      chapter: 36,
      chapterTitle: "Writing for Different Media · Press Releases",
      pages: "287–289",
      pdfPages: "312–314",
      title: "Press Releases: write news for the editor and link it to what everyone is talking about",
      minutes: 7,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 36 · Writing for Different Media · Press Releases · pp. 287–289 (PDF 312–314)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Remember you are appealing to the editor to view what you are sending as news and not a puff piece promoting your company or its product.", page: "288" }
      ],
      idea: "A press release sends information to the media, who publish it at no charge. Free and sometimes very valuable, but **a two-edged sword**: the editor may distort your message in a rewrite. On balance he calls publicity a positive force, usable as your only marketing or as part of a campaign.\n\n**His format:**\n1. At the very top: **“News Release”** (“Press Release” has gotten old hat).\n2. Top right: the date and **“For Immediate Release”** (a later release date is allowed but rare).\n3. A **headline and subheadline** written for the readers of the publication you're sending it to.\n4. A **city dateline**, like a news story: “(Chicago, IL) A major new breakthrough . . .”\n5. Written as a news story with as much detail as possible up front: **who, what, when, where, why and how** at the very beginning, all key points in the first few paragraphs, so an editor gets the gist fast.\n\nThe editor's test is relevance to their audience (quote). It must read as news, not a puff piece. **Quotes** let experts, the company president or customers say things you can't say in the body (his Bone Fone example quoted the maker's chairman), so use them for testimonials and the owner's voice.\n\n**The triggers that matter most: Linking (#14) and Current Fads (#12).** If a fad is sweeping the country, link to it cleanly. In the 1960s his ski-resort client wanted people to know it rented snowmobiles. Tying into the women's liberation movement, he issued a release saying the resort was banning women snowmobile drivers, with a provocative quote from the owner. It made national network news, front pages and talk shows. A week later a second release announced the ban was lifted after complaints, and the resort was opening a school to teach good snowmobiling habits. Rentals jumped. (He admits in Ch 19, p. 151, that these stunts look silly in hindsight. Today a fake sexist ban would be a reputation disaster; keep the *structure* — a newsworthy link to a real trend plus a follow-up story — not the stunt.)\n\nPR can also sell what ads can't: a friend's device that let a cat use the human toilet was refused by every magazine's ad department, but publicity got it national coverage.\n\nHis summary of the copywriter's role: look at your copy the way a news editor would, and write it to fit their idea of a good story.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Publicity is free and powerful, but the editor may rewrite and distort your message",
        "Format: 'News Release,' date + 'For Immediate Release,' headline/subheadline, city dateline",
        "Who, what, when, where, why and how up front; it must read as news, not a puff piece",
        "Attributed quotes say what the body can't: use them for testimonials and the owner's voice",
        "Link to a current fad or trend (triggers #12 and #14) to make it newsworthy",
        "PR can sell what ads can't (the cat-toilet device no magazine would advertise)"
      ],
      why: "Local news, community Facebook groups, podcasts and creator collabs are free reach for PMT and the trades, but only if the pitch reads like a story that editor's audience wants. The same 'write it as news' rule also makes organic posts and UGC-style ads stronger.",
      example: {
        title: "A PMT news release for Motorcycle Safety Awareness Month (May)",
        before: "“PMT is proud to offer the best motorcycle classes in Sacramento! Sign up today!”",
        after: "NEWS RELEASE · [Date] · For Immediate Release\nHeadline: “More First-Time Riders Are Starting After 40, Sacramento Instructors Say”\nSubheadline: “As Motorcycle Safety Awareness Month begins, a local training school reports [real figure] of this spring's beginner students are over 40.”\n(Sacramento, CA) [Who, what, when, where, why, how in two sentences.] “[Owner's own words about why people start later],” said [owner], who has [real credential].",
        body: "News first, a real trend linked to a real awareness month, and the owner's quote doing the selling. Only use figures PMT can back up; if the trend isn't real, find one that is."
      },
      practice: [
        {
          prompt: "Dissect it: take the press-release rules apart.",
          fields: [
            "What is the 'two-edged sword' of PR, and how would you reduce the risk of your message being distorted?",
            "Break down the snowmobile release: which triggers did it use, why did it need a second release, and what would you NOT copy from it today?",
            "Why are attributed quotes so useful in a release?"
          ]
        },
        {
          prompt: "Apply it: draft a one-page news release for PMT or a local trade, linked to a real trend, date or event.",
          fields: [
            "Top block (News Release / date / For Immediate Release)",
            "Headline + subheadline",
            "Dateline + first paragraph (who, what, when, where, why, how)",
            "One attributed quote"
          ],
          rubric: [
            "Reads like news an editor's audience would want, not an ad",
            "All five W's and how in the first paragraph",
            "Linked to a real trend, date or event (no invented controversy)",
            "The quote is attributed and adds what the body can't say"
          ],
          model: "NEWS RELEASE · [Date] · For Immediate Release\n\nHeadline: Sacramento Roofer Offers Free Storm-Damage Photo Checks After [Real Storm Name/Date]\nSubheadline: With insurance claim windows closing in [real timeframe], [Company] is sending crews to photograph roofs in [neighborhoods] at no charge.\n\n(Sacramento, CA) [Company], a [real years]-year-old roofing contractor, will photograph storm damage on homes in [neighborhoods] from [dates] after [storm] brought [real wind/rain figures]. Homeowners book a time at [site]; a crew member sends photos of the roof to their phone the same day, so they can decide whether to file a claim before [deadline].\n\n“Most people can't see their own roof, so they don't know there's a problem until it leaks,” said [owner name], owner of [Company]. [Use the owner's real words.]"
        }
      ],
      today: { task: "List 5 real local news hooks for PMT in the next 90 days (Motorcycle Safety Awareness Month in May, a new class location, a graduate milestone, a seasonal riding story, a student's story) and draft the headline and first paragraph for the best one.", output: "5 news hooks + one draft release opening", minutes: 20 },
      check: [
        { q: "What does Sugarman put at the very top of the page, and why not 'Press Release'?", a: "'News Release'; 'Press Release' has gotten old hat." },
        { q: "Which two triggers play the biggest role in a news release?", a: "Linking (#14) and Current Fads (#12)." },
        { q: "What was the follow-up to the snowmobile 'ban' release?", a: "A second release a week later announcing the ban was lifted after complaints, with the owner saying they'd set up a school to teach good snowmobile habits." }
      ]
