      id: "aw-036m",
      part: "s4",
      chapter: 36,
      chapterTitle: "Writing for Different Media · The Internet Infomercial",
      pages: "304–305",
      pdfPages: "329–330",
      title: "The Internet Infomercial: any length, with an Order button where you ask for the sale",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Chapter 36 · Writing for Different Media · The Internet Infomercial · pp. 304–305 (PDF 329–330)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "The more expensive the product, in general, the longer the infomercial.", page: "305" },
        { text: "Your infomercial need not be expensive.", page: "305" }
      ],
      idea: "The web looks like the ideal home for infomercials: the same audio and video you see on TV, right on the screen, for people interested enough to want all the information they can get.\n\nA TV infomercial runs **28½ minutes**. Online it can be **any length**: run the full TV show, or cut it to 15 minutes. Sugarman gives **four rules**:\n1. **Price.** The more expensive the product, in general, the longer the infomercial (first quote). It's Axiom 11 for video: long enough to get the action.\n2. **Ordering.** Ordering online can be instant. Put an **Order Now button on screen at the moment you'd normally give the call to action**, whether that's in the first five minutes or later. The viewer presses it and orders.\n3. **Attention span.** The web viewer's attention span is much shorter, and leaving is easier than a TV viewer changing channels. TV viewers are used to half-hour blocks and if they switch, they land in the middle of another show, so they stay. Online they can go anywhere: check email, chase something else. So keep the presentation interesting, fast-moving and, in most cases, **shorter than on TV**.\n4. **Upsell, and keep the phone.** After they decide and place the order, do the upsell with audio and video (the back end from aw-036i). And list the **toll-free number**: many people don't feel comfortable ordering on the web and want to talk to a real person.\n\n**It doesn't have to be expensive** (second quote). A good consumer video camera is enough, and the format can be simple, such as you being interviewed by someone. He predicted web infomercials would be one of the fastest-growing segments online and told readers to put them in their media mix.\n\nToday's versions are video sales letters, YouTube ads, long videos on landing pages, webinars and Lives. The four rules translate directly: length by price, a button synced to the spoken call to action, a fast open with frequent changes of pace, a post-purchase video, and a phone or text option for people who want a human. Zach's iPhone or Osmo plus an owner interview is exactly the cheap setup Sugarman describes.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Online, an infomercial can be any length: the full show or a 15-minute cut",
        "Rule 1: the pricier the product, the longer the video",
        "Rule 2: put an Order Now button where the call to action happens",
        "Rule 3: web viewers leave faster than TV viewers, so keep it fast and usually shorter",
        "Rule 4: upsell by video after the order, and show a phone number for people who want a person",
        "It can be cheap: a good camera and a simple interview format"
      ],
      why: "Zach can shoot a PMT or trade 'infomercial' with an iPhone or Osmo in an afternoon. Matching length to price, syncing the button to the call to action and adding a phone number are the rules that make it sell.",
      example: {
        title: "A roof-replacement explainer video",
        before: "A 20-minute uncut walk-through of the company's process, with the booking form only at the bottom of the page.",
        after: "An 8-minute interview: Zach asks the owner the 7 questions homeowners ask most, cut fast with B-roll from real jobs. At minute 2 and minute 7, when the owner invites viewers to book an inspection, a “Book my inspection” button appears under the video. The phone number shows at the end. After booking: a 60-second video from the owner about the gutter-guard add-on [if offered].",
        body: "Length fits a high-ticket decision, the button appears at the call to action, a phone number serves people who want a human, and the upsell comes after the order."
      },
