      title: "Some Final Thoughts: make complex things simple, persist, and teach what you learn",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Epilogue · Some Final Thoughts · pp. 313–316 (PDF 338–341; text ends on p. 315)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "I am not the smartest of men.", page: "313" },
        { text: "The second thing I have is persistence. I don’t easily give up.", page: "314" }
      ],
      idea: "The book is the seminar Sugarman ran from **1977 to 2000**, which students paid up to $3,000 to attend. Many went on to build great companies and fortunes. He congratulates you for the work and hopes you'll use it to contribute to your community and country through advertising that informs, entertains and works.\n\n**Then he takes himself down a peg** (first quote). If he had been smarter he would have made fewer mistakes. Specifically, he would have:\n- **read more** of the direct marketing books (the list in Appendix D),\n- **tested more**,\n- **thought more carefully before risking his money and time.**\n\nHe never graduated from college, his grades weren't great, he nearly flunked English, and he never took an advertising course (which he suspects was his best educational break). He doesn't claim more talent than others either. If you work longer hours and risk more of your time and capital, you eventually learn. He worked and risked a lot, and learned a lot. (Notice Axiom 7 at work: no degree, no ad training, and the 'constraint' turned out not to exist.)\n\n**Two things sum him up:**\n1. **Communication skill:** taking a very complex issue and presenting it in a simple, understandable way. That made him a good teacher, able to pass on *why* things work and the real lessons behind his experiences. He notes that good teachers are rarely good practitioners and vice versa, and he likes to think he's both.\n2. **Persistence** (second quote). He rarely quits, and only for a very good reason. What mattered to him wasn't winning or losing but playing the game hard.\n\n**Why he wrote the book.** There comes a time to step out of the trenches and share what you've learned. He cites Claude Hopkins, who explained in the 1920s that anyone who learns more than others through a lifetime of hard work owes a statement to those who come after. His other motive is a strong desire to give. He learned more at his seminars by giving and sharing than at any other time, because he had to organize and explain his concepts, and then he started following his own advice. Getting to know more than 200 highly motivated participants, their successes and failures, added to his knowledge too: they shared their mistakes as he shared his.\n\nHe closes with Edwin Land's line from Chapter 1, that a mistake is a future benefit whose full value hasn't been realized yet, and says his greatest thrill is helping entrepreneurs build businesses that contribute to their industries and communities. If the book continues that, it's his legacy.",
