      id: "aw-e-appendix-b",
      part: "end",
      chapter: "Appendix B",
      chapterTitle: "Seeds of Curiosity, Continued",
      pages: "319–320",
      pdfPages: "344–345",
      title: "Seeds of Curiosity, Continued: you just jumped to the back of the book",
      minutes: 6,
      tags: ["Book"],
      source: [{ doc: "The Adweek Copywriting Handbook (Joseph Sugarman)", section: "Appendix B · Seeds of Curiosity, Continued · pp. 319–320 (PDF 344–345); links to Chapter 10 · pp. 59–63 (PDF 84–88)", who: "Joseph Sugarman" }],
      quotes: [
        { text: "Your message must always be so compelling that you motivate the reader to do things normally not done.", page: "319" }
      ],
      idea: "In Chapter 10 Sugarman tells the story of **Ginger**, a beautiful beauty-shop owner who came to his office for help, and he breaks off at the most dramatic moment with a note sending you to Appendix B for the ending. Appendix B opens by calling you out: if you couldn't wait and flipped to the back before reading the next line of Chapter 10, you just fell for the seeds-of-curiosity theory in a big way.\n\n**The point** (quote): your message must be so compelling that it makes readers do things they don't normally do, like skipping most of a book to find out how a story ends. He insists it isn't a dirty trick and that the story really happened. And the stakes are real: getting people to reach into their pockets and trade hard-earned money for your product **is not a natural act**. It's one of the hardest things a copywriter does. It takes huge motivation, created by a message so compelling that readers get totally involved (trigger #1), slide down the slippery slide (Axiom 6), and then order, or feel guilty if they haven't (trigger #28).\n\n**The ending, briefly.** Ginger asked him to be her mentor and began to hint that she'd offer herself in return. He stopped her mid-sentence, said he was too busy for outside projects, and offered instead to let her attend his seminar free, on condition that she pay him back after her first million. She left, a little embarrassed, and he never heard from her again. That evening he joked to his wife that he'd almost been seduced in exchange for his copywriting.\n\n**The lessons inside the story** (from Ch 10): Ginger's 50,000-piece mailing returned a tenth of what she needed to break even because (1) she mailed a mail-in offer to a local *retail* list, not mail-order buyers; (2) the copy broke many of the book's principles, so people didn't read it all (low 'traffic'); and (3) she didn't **test** first. She should have mailed 5,000 names, not 50,000.\n\n**How seeds of curiosity work** (Ch 10, Axiom 8): short lines at the end of a paragraph that pull the reader into the next, such as “But there's more,” “So read on,” “Let me explain,” “Now here comes the good part.” Or promise a payoff early that's revealed only at the end (his Consumers Hero ad, Ch 34). The ideal is copy so interesting it doesn't need them, and he warns not to overdo them. Appendix B is the same device at the scale of a whole book, and it only works because the payoff is real.",
      keyPoints: [
        "If you jumped here from Chapter 10, curiosity just made you skip most of the book",
        "A compelling message makes people do things they don't normally do, like ordering from an ad",
        "Parting with money isn't natural; it takes involvement, the slippery slide and a strong reason",
        "The payoff must be real: he insists the story is true and the device isn't a cheat",
        "Ginger's lessons: the right list, readership (traffic), and testing small before mailing big"
      ],
      why: "Open loops drive watch time on Reels and swipe-through on carousels. Sugarman's rule is to make the loop compelling and always pay it off honestly; a fake payoff burns the trust (triggers #2 and #4) the next ad needs.",
      example: {
        title: "An honest open loop for a PMT Reel",
        before: "“Here are 5 tips for new riders!” (No reason to stay past tip 1.)",
        after: "Open: “The thing that scares new riders most isn't speed. I'll tell you what it is at the end, because it surprises everyone.” Middle: three quick, useful tips with “but that's not the big one” between each. End: the real payoff [the instructors' actual answer, e.g. stalling in front of other people], then “and that's exactly what day one is built around.”",
        body: "Seeds of curiosity between sections, a withheld payoff, and a true answer at the end. Use PMT's instructors' real answer, not an invented one."
      },
